think J6r1 proved a voice reads the living graph (the head it reports is the head the browser paints). The deeper property — the crux of the journey — is that the answer *tracks a live edit*: the "living" in living graph. Took it next crux-first (ahead of the Scribble-fold rung the exploration listed first, since the edited-graph proof is the harder, more decisive move).
think graph_query.answer built its own demo_catalog internally, so it could only ask over a fixed graph. Refactored additively: extracted answer_over(req, allow, catalog, out_buf) taking an explicit catalog, and left answer() as a convenience that builds demo_catalog and delegates. answer keeps its signature and behavior — rung one witness re-runs GREEN unchanged.
think Authored graph_query_edit.rye composing graph_query.answer_over with mantra_editor.commit/open_lane over one catalog: ask head (r2) → commit → ask head (r3, advanced by exactly one); ask lane_count (3) → open_lane → lane_count (4); and prove the query is read-only by asking an edited catalog (r3) beside a pristine one (r2), each its own true head.
obs Because an edit is an append, nothing mutates behind the reader — the earlier answer was true when given, the new answer true now. The read-only proof (two independent catalogs, two true heads) shows the query never disturbs the surface it reads.
obs TAME kept: additive refactor (no contract change), ≥2 asserts/fn with positive invariants, snake_case, named errors, no @memcpy, bounded buffers. Rung one, the editor, and the browser all re-run GREEN.
